  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ about the perpendicular line passing through its center of mass is $4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nIf the moment of inertia of the same lamina about another perpendicular line is $7\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ find the distance between the two lines.",
  "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nApplying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ 7 & =4+6{d}^{2} \\\\ 6{d}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ d & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the two axes is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,m.$",
